WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.949 --> 00:00:04.799
There are plenty of ways that filmmakers and
studios curb the content of their work to

00:00:04.799 --> 00:00:06.850
appeal to as large an audience as possible.

00:00:06.850 --> 00:00:11.230
Regardless, there are still some theaters
that choose not to show certain films for

00:00:11.230 --> 00:00:14.980
a variety of reasons, whether they be moral,
fiscal, or logistical.

00:00:14.980 --> 00:00:18.640
Here are a just a few examples of times theaters
backed out from showing a film.

00:00:18.640 --> 00:00:24.070
Perhaps the most popular of the recent films
to be refused by a movie theater is 2017's

00:00:24.070 --> 00:00:25.430
Star Wars: The Last Jedi.

00:00:25.430 --> 00:00:29.890
A small theater in Iowa decided not to screen
this entry in the Star Wars franchise, but

00:00:29.890 --> 00:00:33.140
not because its owners objected to the content
of the film.

00:00:33.140 --> 00:00:36.290
Instead, it was because of an issue with Disney's
terms.

00:00:36.290 --> 00:00:40.760
As part of their distribution deal, Disney
required theaters to screen The Last Jedi

00:00:40.760 --> 00:00:46.040
in their largest auditoriums for a minimum
of four weeks, and took a cut of 65% of ticket

00:00:46.040 --> 00:00:47.820
revenue from those screenings.

00:00:47.820 --> 00:00:52.140
That's a high price to pay for any small-town
movie theater, especially in recent years,

00:00:52.140 --> 00:00:56.760
when plenty of potential audience members
have ditched the big screen for lots of smaller

00:00:56.760 --> 00:00:59.000
screens that stream entertainment at home.

00:00:59.000 --> 00:01:02.800
Of course, The Last Jedi still went on to
grab an international box office total of

00:01:02.800 --> 00:01:07.600
more than $1 billion, even without being shown
in the Keystone of Northeast Iowa.

00:01:07.600 --> 00:01:12.109
Not all theater cancellations are the product
of inter-company conflict.

00:01:12.109 --> 00:01:13.689
Some are a matter of public safety.

00:01:13.689 --> 00:01:18.659
The 2014 comedy The Interview, directed and
produced by Seth Rogen and Evan Goldberg,

00:01:18.659 --> 00:01:22.969
was the subject of some headline-grabbing
controversy because its plot is centered around

00:01:22.969 --> 00:01:27.789
the assassination of Kim Jong-Un, the current
real-life Supreme Leader of North Korea.

00:01:27.789 --> 00:01:31.359
When word got out about the movie's premise,
a group of hackers calling themselves the

00:01:31.359 --> 00:01:35.570
"Guardians of Peace" threatened that tragedy
would befall any theater that screened the

00:01:35.570 --> 00:01:39.789
film, causing Rogen and actor James Franco
to cancel publicity appearances.

00:01:39.800 --> 00:01:44.680
"Haters gonna hate…and ainters gona ain’t"

00:01:44.680 --> 00:01:47.720
"That is not an actual thing people say."

00:01:47.729 --> 00:01:52.869
Not long after this announcement, Carmike,
AMC, Regal, and Cineplex all stated that they

00:01:52.869 --> 00:01:57.499
would not be screening the film, citing the
safety of their patrons as their top priority.

00:01:57.499 --> 00:02:02.819
With so many theaters shutting out The Interview,
Sony chose to pull its cinematic release altogether,

00:02:02.819 --> 00:02:06.859
though they were met with some criticism from
then-President Barack Obama for caving in

00:02:06.859 --> 00:02:08.360
to terrorist threats.

00:02:08.360 --> 00:02:13.400
In the summer of 2018, a Wisconsin theater
company chose not to screen the Slender Man

00:02:13.409 --> 00:02:16.629
film because its violence hit a little too
close to home.

00:02:16.629 --> 00:02:21.459
As many moviegoers undoubtedly already knew,
Slender Man is a fictional character born

00:02:21.459 --> 00:02:26.790
out of a meme on the Something Awful forums
in 2009, a cryptid for the modern world.

00:02:26.790 --> 00:02:32.250
In 2014, two 12-year-old Wisconsin girls,
allegedly in tribute to this fearsome fictional

00:02:32.250 --> 00:02:35.459
being, attacked one of their friends, nearly
killing her.

00:02:35.459 --> 00:02:39.390
Because of the severity and premeditated nature
of the crime, the girls were tried as adults

00:02:39.390 --> 00:02:41.599
and charged with first-degree attempted homicide.

00:02:41.599 --> 00:02:46.030
Though the victim survived, this horrifying
tragedy has surely left its mark on people

00:02:46.030 --> 00:02:47.030
from the area.

00:02:47.030 --> 00:02:50.549
The theater company chose to withhold the
screening out of respect for the victim, and

00:02:50.560 --> 00:02:53.120
given the circumstances, it's hard to blame
them.

00:02:53.160 --> 00:02:57.920
Some film controversies are not as life-threatening
as terrorist attacks, but still manage to

00:02:57.939 --> 00:03:00.000
keep some movies out of theaters.

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:03.799
Such is the case of a drive-in theater in
Alabama that refused to show the live action

00:03:03.799 --> 00:03:08.379
version of Disney's Beauty and the Beast after
the director revealed that the character LeFou,

00:03:08.379 --> 00:03:10.750
Gaston's lackey, is gay.

00:03:10.750 --> 00:03:15.159
The proprietors of the cinema implied that
homosexuality was inappropriate for all-age

00:03:15.159 --> 00:03:19.400
audiences, and that it was a sin, citing their
Christian faith as first and foremost in their

00:03:19.400 --> 00:03:20.940
decision-making process.

00:03:20.940 --> 00:03:25.439
Ironically, the film also got complaints from
some viewers because, after headlines touted

00:03:25.439 --> 00:03:29.999
LeFou as Disney's first openly gay character,
any content that might've dealt with that

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:33.360
was pretty much limited to the subtext that's
basically in every movie.

00:03:33.360 --> 00:03:38.160
"I'm not done with you yet."

00:03:38.160 --> 00:03:40.239
"Me neither."

00:03:40.239 --> 00:03:44.239
Whether or not LeFou can be considered an
example of decent gay representation, it is

00:03:44.239 --> 00:03:48.950
unfortunate that this theater chose to discriminate
based on the sexuality of a fictional character

00:03:48.950 --> 00:03:52.980
and send a clear message to their patrons
about who they do and do not approve of in

00:03:52.980 --> 00:03:53.980
their establishment.

00:03:53.980 --> 00:03:57.959
Perhaps unsurprisingly, since this story was
covered by national news outlets, the theater

00:03:57.960 --> 00:03:59.600
has since changed management.

00:03:59.600 --> 00:04:04.389
Religious fervor has always been a cause for
controversy in art and media, and you can

00:04:04.389 --> 00:04:09.930
see a pretty clear example of how with Monty
Python's religious satire, Life of Brian.

00:04:09.930 --> 00:04:12.540
"By what name are you calling him?”

00:04:12.540 --> 00:04:13.540
“Ah Brian!"

00:04:13.540 --> 00:04:19.010
"We worship you o Brian, who are lord over
us all."

00:04:19.010 --> 00:04:22.920
The British comedy classic is centered on
a young Jewish man who was born on the same

00:04:22.920 --> 00:04:27.640
day as Jesus Christ who gets mistaken for
the Messiah and is eventually crucified in

00:04:27.640 --> 00:04:31.260
a scene set to a cheerful tune about looking
on the bright side of life.

00:04:31.260 --> 00:04:35.640
It probably won't shock you to learn that
upon its release in 1979, it was actually

00:04:35.640 --> 00:04:39.130
banned in Ireland and Norway because of its
blasphemous themes.

00:04:39.130 --> 00:04:43.450
The film itself sets out to criticize the
misunderstandings and pitfalls of modern organized

00:04:43.450 --> 00:04:47.790
religion rather than ridiculing faith, but
the movie still drew plenty of criticism for

00:04:47.790 --> 00:04:49.640
its irreverent treatment of the subject.

00:04:49.640 --> 00:04:54.600
Nether the criticism nor the ban stopped Life
of Brian from racking up an outstanding box

00:04:54.600 --> 00:04:58.280
office total in the United States and securing
a legacy among fans.

00:04:58.280 --> 00:05:04.080
A 40th anniversary reissue saw over 400 screenings
across the United States and United Kingdom.

00:05:04.080 --> 00:05:08.840
A more serious film that drew inspiration
from Christianity, and an even more intense

00:05:08.860 --> 00:05:13.970
ire from Christian movie-goers, was 1988's
The Last Temptation of Christ.

00:05:13.970 --> 00:05:18.780
Directed by the legendary Martin Scorsese
this adaptation of the 1955 novel of the same

00:05:18.780 --> 00:05:24.600
name depicts Jesus, played by Willem Dafoe,
imagining himself entangled in situations

00:05:24.600 --> 00:05:27.950
that conflict with certain Biblical depictions
of Christ.

00:05:27.950 --> 00:05:33.290
Upon its release, an organization called Mastermedia
International incited a boycott, which led

00:05:33.290 --> 00:05:38.450
to coverage on Christian radio stations, protests,
and eventually three U.S. theater companies

00:05:38.450 --> 00:05:40.290
deciding not to screen it at all.

00:05:40.290 --> 00:05:41.750
They might've made the right decision.

00:05:41.750 --> 00:05:46.550
In Paris, a screening of the film was interrupted
when a fundamentalist Catholic group set fire

00:05:46.550 --> 00:05:50.220
to the theater while it was playing, injuring
13 patrons.

